# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
    "base": [
        ("Telling a stretch wrinkle from a settled base",
         "<p>Two different problems both show up as a bump in the turf, and a {city} homeowner usually can't tell them apart from the porch. A wrinkle from turf that was never stretched drum-tight tends to run as one long ridge across a panel, staying the same shape season to season. A wrinkle from a base that's settled underneath tends to sit lower on one side, spreads a little wider each rainy season, and often comes with a soft or slightly spongy feel underfoot at the low point. Lifting the corner and checking the base itself, rather than judging by the wrinkle's shape alone, is the only reliable way to know which one is causing a given call.</p>"),
        ("When a washed-out edge is the real cause of a lifted corner",
         "<p>A downspout emptying near a turf edge, or a grade that pitches water toward one corner of a {city} yard instead of away from it, can erode the base right under the perimeter over a few rainy seasons, long before the visible edge itself ever lifts. What looks like a nail simply pulling loose is often that eroded gap letting the edge flex more than it should under normal foot traffic or wind. Reseating the nails without rebuilding the washed-out section underneath fixes the symptom for a matter of weeks, since the material that held them originally is no longer there to hold them again.</p>"),
        ("Fines-crusted base from an older, pre-standard install",
         "<p>Turf put down before Florida adopted its current subgrade standard was sometimes built over material that was never washed first, and unwashed fines bind into a crust over years that blocks water from passing through the way the base was supposed to. A {city} lawn built this way can look fine on the surface for a decade and still hold water at one low spot every time it rains, a spot that keeps reopening as a repair no matter how many times the visible symptom gets patched. Correcting that means breaking up and replacing the crusted section, not just resealing the turf above it.</p>"),
        ("A capped irrigation head that was never fully removed",
         "<p>Older installs sometimes capped an irrigation head at the surface without pulling the pipe and fitting below it, and that buried hardware can corrode and collapse into a small void over several years, leaving a soft spot that shows up as a dip in the turf above it. On a {city} property with a lawn more than a decade old, a recurring low spot in roughly the same place a sprinkler zone used to run is worth checking for exactly this before assuming the base simply settled on its own. Digging down to the old fitting and filling the void properly stops the dip from reappearing after each repair.</p>"),
        ("How a live oak root shows itself differently than a settled base",
         "<p>A base that's settled generally produces a broad, shallow dip, while a root working its way under a {city} lawn tends to heave a narrower ridge that follows a fairly straight line, often tracking back toward the trunk of a nearby tree. The two causes call for different fixes: a settled area gets built back up and recompacted, while a root-heaved section needs the root itself assessed, sometimes with an arborist's input, before deciding whether to reroute the turf's edge or accept that the same spot will need attention again as the root keeps growing.</p>"),
    ],
    "product": [
        ("Why grain direction gets checked before a patch is cut",
         "<p>Every turf product has a pile that leans slightly in one direction from manufacturing, and a patch seamed in with its grain running the opposite way from the surrounding turf on a {city} lawn will show as a visible color break under low-angle morning or evening light, even if the product itself is an exact match. Checking grain direction against the existing lawn before cutting a replacement piece, not after it's already seamed in, is what keeps a repaired section from standing out under exactly the lighting conditions a homeowner is most likely to notice it in.</p>"),
        ("Dye lots and why an exact SKU still isn't a perfect match",
         "<p>Turf color varies slightly between production runs even within the same product line, the way carpet or paint can shift a shade between batches, so ordering the identical model number for a {city} repair doesn't guarantee an invisible patch. A small difference that's unnoticeable on a sample card can read as a faint rectangle once it's seamed into an existing lawn that's had a season or two of sun on it already. Placing a patch where the color break falls along a natural line, a fence shadow or a planting bed edge, does more to hide the difference than chasing an exact dye lot ever will.</p>"),
        ("Sun exposure changes how visible a patch ends up",
         "<p>A patch on the shaded north side of a {city} house tends to blend into the surrounding turf far better than the same repair would on a section that gets full afternoon sun, since UV fade moves faster on exposed turf and slower in shade, widening the color gap between old and new material over time. A repair scheduled soon after a problem appears, before the surrounding turf has faded much further, generally blends in far better than the same patch put off for another year or two of sun exposure on an already-aging lawn.</p>"),
        ("Matching backing and infill spec, not just the visible blade",
         "<p>A patch on a pet run needs the same fully permeable backing and odor-control infill the rest of that {city} run was built with, not a standard residential product that happens to be on hand, since a mismatched backing section behaves differently under a hose rinse even if the blade color matches perfectly. The same logic applies to a putting green repair, where the pile height and density driving ball roll matter more than color. Matching the functional spec, not only the look, is what keeps a repaired section performing the same as the rest of the surface around it.</p>"),
        ("Choosing the closest pile height when the original is gone",
         "<p>Some turf products get discontinued well before a {city} lawn reaches the age where it needs a patch, and when that happens the closest available pile height and density, not an exact match, is what a repair actually works with. A slight height difference is easiest to place somewhere it won't draw attention, tucked near a planting bed or under an area furniture usually covers, rather than dead center in an open section of lawn. Being upfront about that tradeoff before the work starts avoids a surprise once the patch is finished and compared against the surrounding turf.</p>"),
    ],
    "process": [
        ("What a photo can and can't tell us before a visit",
         "<p>A clear, well-lit photo of an open seam or a wrinkle, taken from a few feet back with another shot close up, is usually enough for us to give a {city} homeowner a preliminary read on scope before scheduling anything. What a photo can't show is the condition of the base underneath, so that first estimate is always a range rather than a firm number, and it can move once someone's actually lifted the turf on site. Sending photos first still saves a visit in the cases where the problem turns out to be smaller than it looked in a phone picture.</p>"),
        ("Why the site visit sometimes changes the scope entirely",
         "<p>Once we're on site at a {city} property and lift the corner of a suspected wrinkle or seam, the base underneath sometimes tells a different story than the surface did, a soft spot that wasn't visible in any photo, or a root that's clearly still growing under the turf. That's the point of the visit rather than quoting off photos alone: a repair scoped only for the visible symptom can miss the actual cause and fail again within a season. Confirming what's underneath before cutting anything is standard on every call, not an upsell added once the crew arrives.</p>"),
        ("Cutting a patch to follow the panel, not just the damage",
         "<p>Rather than cutting a patch shaped tightly around the exact damaged area, we usually cut along the nearest existing seam or a straight line that follows the turf's own panel layout on a {city} lawn, since a patch outline that matches how the turf was originally pieced together reads as an intentional seam rather than an obvious repair scar. This takes a little more turf than the strict minimum the damage requires, but it trades a small amount of extra material for an outline that's far less noticeable once it's seamed and brushed in.</p>"),
        ("How long a seam actually needs to cure in Florida humidity",
         "<p>Seam adhesive on a {city} repair needs a few hours to cure fully, and Florida's humidity can slow that slightly compared with a drier climate, which is why we weight a fresh seam with sandbags or boards rather than assuming it's ready the moment the adhesive looks dry on top. Rushing this step by walking across a seam too soon, or letting a sudden afternoon shower hit it before it's cured, is one of the more common reasons a professionally repaired seam opens again within the first few weeks instead of holding for years.</p>"),
        ("Scheduling repair work around the rainy season's storm pattern",
         "<p>We try to schedule seam and edge repairs for the morning on a {city} job during the June-through-September rainy season, since afternoon storms arrive predictably enough that gluing a seam with only an hour or two of dry cure time before rain hits is a real risk in summer. A repair pushed into a rain-free morning window holds up better than the same work squeezed in ahead of a forecast storm, which is part of why scheduling flexibility matters more for a repair visit in summer than it does the rest of the year.</p>"),
    ],
    "risks": [
        ("A repeat melt patch means the glare was never fixed",
         "<p>Patching a melted section of turf without addressing what caused it, usually a low-E window or reflective surface bouncing concentrated sun onto that exact spot, means the new patch sits in the same 175- to 200-degree hot spot the old turf did. On a {city} lawn with a west- or south-facing low-E window nearby, that patch can start to soften within another season or two of full sun, in the same shape and location as before. Repositioning the affected turf, adding a film to the glass, or shading that section is what actually stops the cycle, not a second patch alone.</p>"),
        ("Reseaming without correcting the water source",
         "<p>A seam that opened because water was standing against it, from a low spot or a grade that never sloped away properly, will reopen again if the repair only reglues the seam without also correcting the grade underneath. On a {city} lawn, that usually means lifting a wider area than the seam itself, regrading to restore the one to two percent slope, and recompacting before the turf goes back down. Skipping that step to save time on a single visit is the most common reason the same seam generates a second service call within a year.</p>"),
        ("An old fines-crusted base undermining a fresh reseam",
         "<p>Reseaming turf over a base that was never washed properly in the first place looks fine on install day, since the new tape and adhesive bond correctly to whatever surface is there. The problem shows up later on a {city} lawn when that crusted, poorly draining base keeps shifting slightly with every heavy rain, working the fresh seam loose from underneath even though the seam work itself was done correctly. Identifying an unwashed, crusted base during the site visit, rather than after a second failure, is what keeps a repair from becoming a repeat call.</p>"),
        ("A live oak root that keeps growing after the patch",
         "<p>A root heaving a seam or panel from below doesn't stop growing just because a repair crew cut out and reseamed the affected section, so a {city} lawn with a live oak close to the turf's edge can see the same general area lift again within a year or two even after a clean repair. Rerouting the turf's edge farther from the root, or accepting a shorter repair interval in that specific spot, are the realistic options once a root is confirmed as the cause, since the tree itself isn't something a turf repair addresses.</p>"),
        ("Fixing the visible spot while missing the one next to it",
         "<p>A storm that lifts one corner of a {city} lawn's perimeter has usually put the same wind load on the rest of the edge, even where nothing has visibly failed yet. Repairing only the obviously lifted section and skipping a full perimeter check means a second, marginally weaker spot nearby can fail on the very next storm, turning one repair call into two spread a few weeks apart. Walking the entire edge while already on site for one repair catches that second weak point while it's still an easy fix rather than a fresh emergency.</p>"),
    ],
    "care": [
        ("Watching a fresh seam through its first real storm",
         "<p>Cure time under weight tells us a seam is set, but the first heavy rain after a {city} repair is the actual test of whether it holds under real water flow rather than just foot traffic. We suggest checking the repaired section specifically after the next storm rather than waiting for the regular seasonal walk-around, since a seam that's going to have a problem usually shows the first sign, a slightly raised edge or a thin gap, within that first significant rain rather than months later.</p>"),
        ("Why new infill at a patch settles differently at first",
         "<p>Infill brushed into a fresh patch on a {city} lawn hasn't gone through a rainy season yet, so it tends to settle and compact a little more than infill in the surrounding turf during the first few months after a repair. Checking the patch area specifically, rather than assuming it matches the rest of the lawn's infill depth, catches a thin spot early enough to top it up before it becomes visible as a slightly different shade or texture where the repair meets the older turf around it.</p>"),
        ("Keeping a dog away from a freshly patched dig spot",
         "<p>A repair near a gate or fence line where a dog has been digging needs a short window of managed access on a {city} property, since the same spot that failed once is exactly where a dog is likely to return to first. A temporary barrier or closer supervision for the day or two while the adhesive fully cures protects the new seam from the one kind of stress, direct digging, that a repair in that location is most at risk from before it's had time to set completely.</p>"),
        ("Timing aftercare to the season a repair happens in",
         "<p>A repair done during Florida's dry season has more margin before the next heavy rain arrives, so a {city} homeowner can generally go about a normal routine sooner after the work is finished. A repair completed during the June-through-September rainy season needs closer attention in that first week or two, since the next storm may arrive within days rather than weeks, and that narrower window is when a marginal seam or edge is most likely to show an early problem worth calling about.</p>"),
        ("Catching a repair loosening before it fully reopens",
         "<p>A seam or edge on the verge of failing again usually gives a small signal first, a corner that lifts slightly underfoot, a faint gap where tape used to sit flush, before it fully separates. Checking a repaired area on a {city} lawn every few weeks rather than only after it's visibly reopened means catching that early signal while it's a five-minute fix instead of waiting for the same spot to need a full second repair visit months down the line.</p>"),
    ],
}
